          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I was told my thyroid is underactive.",
          "answer": "An underactive thyroid, or hypothyroidism, means the thyroid gland does not make enough thyroid hormone. The gland makes mainly thyroxine, which the body converts into a more active hormone, triiodothyronine. Together these set the body's metabolic rate, meaning how fast many tissues work. (Source: *Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThe pituitary gland, a small gland at the base of the brain, monitors thyroid hormone levels. When levels fall, it releases more thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH). That is why TSH is usually the first blood test: it often rises before the thyroid hormone level itself goes out of range. (Same source.)\n\nHypothyroidism is classified by where the fault lies. In primary hypothyroidism the thyroid gland itself is at fault. It accounts for most cases. In central hypothyroidism the pituitary or hypothalamus is at fault. This is uncommon, and its blood test pattern is different. (Same source.)\n\nFor coding, ICD-10-CM places thyroid disorders, including hypothyroidism, in Chapter 4, endocrine, nutritional and metabolic diseases, at codes E00 to E07. The passages I retrieved did not give the exact code for hypothyroidism, so please check the precise code with the clinician or coder who gave you the diagnosis. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 4*, US clinical modification summary.)\n\nI could not retrieve guidance on lifestyle, diet, activity, or day-to-day management. My lifestyle lookup failed, so I cannot give you a sourced answer on those points. Useful questions to bring to your clinician include:\n\n- Which type of hypothyroidism I have, and what caused it.\n- Whether I need further tests, and how often my levels will be checked.\n- What symptoms should prompt me to contact them sooner.\n- Whether any diet, activity, or timing changes matter for my situation.\n\nIf you develop severe symptoms such as chest pain, fainting, severe breathlessness, or sudden confusion, seek urgent medical care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
